<!-- section break: preliminary pages end, main body (arabic numbers) begin=
s --></span></b>

<h1><span style=3D'mso-fareast-font-family:"Times New Roman";mso-font-kerni=
ng:
18.0pt'><!-- =3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=
=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=
=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D --><!-- SECTION 2: MAIN BODY =
(ARABIC NUMERALS FROM CHAPTER ONE)        --><!-- =3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=
=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=
=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=
=3D=3D -->Chapter
One: Introduction<o:p></o:p></span></h1>

<h2><span style=3D'mso-fareast-font-family:"Times New Roman"'>1.1 Backgroun=
d of
the study<o:p></o:p></span></h2>

<p>The real estate sector is one of the fastest growing industries in Somal=
ia,
driven by rapid urbanisation, a large diaspora community investing in prope=
rty,
and the continuous expansion of residential and commercial developments in
cities such as Garowe, Bosaso, and Mogadishu. Real estate companies in this
environment are typically responsible for several interrelated activities at
the same time: planning and monitoring construction projects, maintaining an
inventory of individual properties within each project, recording sales and
rental agreements with customers, tracking instalment and rent payments over
long periods, and producing management reports that support pricing and
investment decisions.</p>

<p>In many small and medium-sized real estate companies operating locally,
these activities are still coordinated using a combination of paper files,
register books, and general-purpose spreadsheet programs such as Microsoft
Excel. Although spreadsheets are flexible and familiar to office staff, they
were not designed to enforce the business rules that a real estate company
depends on, for example, ensuring that a property cannot be sold twice, or =
that
a customer's outstanding balance is always consistent with the payments that
have actually been received. As a company grows and the number of active
projects, properties, and customers increases, spreadsheet-based tracking
becomes increasingly difficult to maintain, and the risk of duplicated reco=
rds,
lost files, and calculation errors increases correspondingly.</p>

<p>Advances in web application frameworks and relational database technology
now make it realistic for a company of modest size to adopt a dedicated,
purpose-built information system rather than continuing to adapt
general-purpose office tools to specialised business needs. Modern framewor=
ks
such as ASP.NET Core, combined with mature object-relational mapping tools =
such
as Entity Framework Core and reliable database engines such as Microsoft SQL
Server, allow a single developer or a small team to build a secure, role-aw=
are,
multi-user system within an academic project timeline. This thesis documents
the design and implementation of such a system, referred to throughout as t=
he
Real Estate Project Management System, which was built as the practical
component of this Bachelor of Science degree in Computer Science.</p>

<p>The system that resulted from this work centralises project, property,
customer, sales, rental, payment, document, and reporting functions into a
single web application, accessible to different categories of staff accordi=
ng
to clearly defined roles. It was designed with a real, small real estate
company's day-to-day operations in mind, drawing on informal observation of=
 how
such companies currently keep records, and on the functional gaps that this
observation revealed.</p>

<h2><span style=3D'mso-fareast-font-family:"Times New Roman"'>1.2 Problem
statement<o:p></o:p></span></h2>

<p>Real estate companies that rely on manual or spreadsheet-based record
keeping face a recurring set of operational problems. First, property status
information is often out of date, because there is no single authoritative
record that is automatically updated the moment a property is sold or rente=
d;
sales agents may therefore continue to market a property that a colleague h=
as
already sold, resulting in confusion and lost customer confidence. Second, =
payment
tracking is largely manual, so outstanding balances on sales and rental
contracts must be recalculated by hand whenever a new payment is received,
which is time-consuming and susceptible to arithmetic mistakes. Third, ther=
e is
no automatic mechanism to warn management about payments that are overdue,
contracts that are close to expiry, or construction projects that are falli=
ng
behind schedule, so such issues are frequently discovered only after they h=
ave
already caused financial or reputational harm. Fourth, important documents =
such
as sales contracts, rental agreements, and payment receipts are stored in
scattered physical folders or personal computers, making them difficult to
retrieve quickly when needed for an audit, a customer dispute, or a managem=
ent
review.</p>

<p>Taken together, these problems mean that decision-makers in a growing re=
al
estate company cannot rely on their existing tools to answer, at any given
moment, simple but important questions: how many properties remain available
for sale, how much revenue has been collected against how much is still
outstanding, which contracts require urgent attention, and how each ongoing
project is progressing against its planned schedule and budget. The central
problem addressed by this study is therefore the absence of an integrated,
rule-enforcing information system that can answer these questions accurately
and immediately, while remaining simple enough for non-technical staff to u=
se
on a daily basis.</p>

<h2><span style=3D'mso-fareast-font-family:"Times New Roman"'>1.3 Objective=
s of
the study<o:p></o:p></span></h2>

<h3><span style=3D'mso-fareast-font-family:"Times New Roman"'>1.3.1 General
objective<o:p></o:p></span></h3>

<p>The general objective of this study is to design, develop, and evaluate a
web-based Real Estate Project Management System that digitises and integrat=
es
the core project, property, sales, rental, payment, and reporting operation=
s of
a small to medium-sized real estate company.</p>

<h3><span style=3D'mso-fareast-font-family:"Times New Roman"'>1.3.2 Specific
objectives<o:p></o:p></span></h3>

<p>The study was guided by the following specific objectives.</p>

<p>One, to review and document the shortcomings of the existing manual and
spreadsheet-based methods used by real estate companies to manage projects,
properties, and customer transactions.</p>

<p>Two, to design a relational database schema and a role-based system
architecture capable of supporting the five categories of user identified
during requirements analysis, namely Administrator, Project Manager, Sales
Agent, Accountant, and Customer.</p>

<p>Three, to implement a functional web application that automates property
status updates, sales and rental record keeping, payment balance recalculat=
ion,
document storage, and system-generated notifications.</p>

<p>Four, to test the resulting system through unit-level verification of its
core business rules and structured user acceptance testing, so as to confirm
that it meets the objectives stated above.</p>

<h2><span style=3D'mso-fareast-font-family:"Times New Roman"'>1.4 Scope and
limitations of the study<o:p></o:p></span></h2>

<p>The system developed in this study covers project management, property
inventory management, customer relationship records, sales transactions, re=
ntal
contracts, payment processing and balance tracking, document storage and
retrieval, dashboard reporting, and role-based user administration. It supp=
orts
five user roles, namely Administrator, Project Manager, Sales Agent,
Accountant, and Customer, each restricted to the functions relevant to that
role through the authorisation features of ASP.NET Core Identity. The system
was designed and tested as a single-organisation, single-branch application
intended to run on a local or single-instance server deployment.</p>

<p>Several areas were deliberately left outside the scope of this study. The
system does not include a public-facing property listing website for prospe=
ctive
buyers who are not yet registered customers, nor does it provide integration
with external payment gateways, mobile money application programming
interfaces, or short message service and electronic mail providers, although
the architecture allows such integrations to be added in future work.
Multi-branch or multi-company support, in which a single deployment would s=
erve
several independent real estate companies with isolated data, was likewise =
not
implemented. Finally, because the system was developed and evaluated within=
 an
academic timeframe using demonstration data rather than a live production
dataset, its performance under a very large number of concurrent users has =
not
been verified and is identified as an area for future work in Chapter Six.<=
/p>

<h2><span style=3D'mso-fareast-font-family:"Times New Roman"'>1.5 Significa=
nce of
the study<o:p></o:p></span></h2>

<p>This study is significant to several groups of stakeholders. For real es=
tate
company owners and managers, the system offers a practical, low-cost
alternative to expensive commercial property management platforms, while st=
ill
enforcing the core business rules that protect the company from double-sell=
ing
properties or losing track of outstanding balances. For sales agents,
accountants, and project managers, the system removes much of the manual
arithmetic and cross-checking that their daily tasks currently require,
allowing them to focus on customer service and project delivery instead of
record keeping. For customers, the system creates the foundation for more
transparent and timely communication regarding the status of their purchase=
 or
rental agreement.</p>

<p>From an academic perspective, this study demonstrates the practical
application of software engineering principles, relational database design,=
 and
modern web development technology to a real, locally relevant business prob=
lem,
in fulfilment of the practical project requirement of the Bachelor of Scien=
ce
degree in Computer Science at East Africa University &#8211; Garowe. It also
contributes a documented case study that future students in the Faculty of
Technology and Engineering may reference when designing information systems=
 for
other sectors of the local economy.</p>

<h2><span style=3D'mso-fareast-font-family:"Times New Roman"'>1.6 Organisat=
ion of
the thesis<o:p></o:p></span></h2>

<p>This thesis is organised into six chapters. Chapter One has introduced t=
he
background, problem statement, objectives, scope, and significance of the
study. Chapter Two reviews related literature and existing property managem=
ent
technologies, and identifies the research gap that this study addresses.
Chapter Three describes the research methodology, the tools and technologies
used, and the system design approach adopted during development. Chapter Fo=
ur
presents the system analysis and design, including the functional and
non-functional requirements, system architecture, use case diagram, entity
relationship diagram, and database design. Chapter Five documents the
implementation and testing of the system, supported by annotated screenshot=
s of
the working application. Chapter Six concludes the thesis with a summary of
findings, recommendations, and suggested directions for future enhancement.=
</p>

<h1><span style=3D'mso-fareast-font-family:"Times New Roman";mso-font-kerni=
ng:
18.0pt'>Chapter Two: Literature Review<o:p></o:p></span></h1>

<h2><span style=3D'mso-fareast-font-family:"Times New Roman"'>2.1 Review of
related studies<o:p></o:p></span></h2>

<p>Software engineering literature has long emphasised that information sys=
tems
perform best when they are built around a clear understanding of the busine=
ss
rules of the domain they serve, rather than being a direct digital copy of =
the
paper forms they replace (Sommerville, 2016). This principle is particularly
relevant to real estate management, where a small number of business rules,
such as the fact that a single property cannot simultaneously be available,
sold, and rented, must be enforced consistently across every part of the sy=
stem
rather than left to the discipline of individual staff members. Pressman and
Maxim (2020) similarly argue that requirements engineering activities,
including stakeholder observation and iterative validation, are essential
before design work begins, because errors introduced at the requirements st=
age
are the most expensive to correct once a system has been implemented.</p>

<p>Database-oriented literature reinforces this view from a data modelling
perspective. Elmasri and Navathe (2016) describe how a well-normalised
relational schema, together with foreign key constraints, can enforce
referential integrity rules that would otherwise depend on manual disciplin=
e,
for example guaranteeing that a payment record can never exist without a
corresponding sale or rental agreement. This principle directly informed the
database design decisions documented in Chapter Four of this thesis, where
relationships between the Property, Sale, Rental, Payment, and Customer
entities were deliberately constrained at the database level rather than
relying solely on application-level checks.</p>

<p>On the architectural side, Fowler (2003) documents the Repository patter=
n as
a means of separating an application's business logic from the details of h=
ow
data is persisted, which improves testability and allows the underlying data
access technology to change with limited impact on the rest of the system. =
This
pattern, together with a layered service architecture, is reflected in the
architecture of the system developed in this study, described in detail in
Section 4.2.</p>

<h2><span style=3D'mso-fareast-font-family:"Times New Roman"'>2.2 Existing
systems and technologies<o:p></o:p></span></h2>

<p>Several commercial property and real estate management platforms are
available internationally, and reviewing their capabilities helps to positi=
on
the contribution of this study. Yardi Voyager and AppFolio Property Manager=
 are
widely used cloud-based platforms that provide accounting, leasing, and
maintenance-tracking functionality for large property portfolios, while
Buildium targets small and medium-sized property managers with similar, tho=
ugh
less extensive, functionality. These platforms are generally well engineered
and feature-rich, but they are priced and licensed for markets with reliabl=
e,
continuous internet connectivity and established banking integrations, and =
they
are typically not tailored to the specific workflow of a real estate develo=
per
that both constructs projects and subsequently sells or rents the resulting
units, as is common in the Somali market.</p>

<p>Beyond commercial software, the wider PropTech, meaning property technol=
ogy,
movement has been documented extensively; Baum (2020) traces how digital
platforms have progressively been adopted across the property lifecycle, fr=
om
marketing and transactions through to ongoing management, and observes that
smaller markets and smaller companies frequently lag behind in adoption bec=
ause
available tools are not adapted to their scale or their local business prac=
tices.
This observation is consistent with what was found during the
requirements-gathering stage of this study, described in Chapter Three, whe=
re
the real estate offices consulted continued to rely on spreadsheets
specifically because available commercial alternatives were considered too
costly or too complex for their size of operation.</p>

<p>In the academic domain, numerous undergraduate and graduate information
systems projects have addressed related problems, such as generic property
listing websites or simple rental booking systems. However, such projects
typically focus on a single transaction type, for example either sales or
rentals, and rarely integrate project-level construction tracking, multi-ro=
le
staff access, automatic payment recalculation, and system-generated
notifications within a single coherent application, which is the combinatio=
n of
features that this study set out to deliver.</p>

<h2><span style=3D'mso-fareast-font-family:"Times New Roman"'>2.3 Research =
gap<o:p></o:p></span></h2>

<p>The review above identifies a gap between, on one hand, feature-rich but
costly commercial platforms that are not adapted to the scale or workflow o=
f a
small local developer-cum-agency, and, on the other hand, narrowly scoped
academic prototypes that typically implement only one transaction type in
isolation. Neither category adequately addresses the case of a company that
both develops real estate projects and subsequently manages the resulting
properties through sale or rental, tracks staged payments against each
transaction, and requires role-based access for distinct categories of staf=
f,
all within a system that is inexpensive enough to deploy on a single local
server. This study addresses that gap by combining project tracking, proper=
ty
inventory, sales, rentals, payments, document management, and role-based ac=
cess
control into a single, integrated web application built entirely on freely
available, industry-standard technology.</p>

<h2><span style=3D'mso-fareast-font-family:"Times New Roman"'>2.4 Conceptual
framework<o:p></o:p></span></h2>

<p>The conceptual framework underlying this study treats the real estate
company as a set of interconnected processes rather than a collection of
independent record books. A construction project produces one or more
properties; each property may, at any given time, be available, reserved, u=
nder
construction, sold, or rented; a sale or a rental agreement, once created,
generates an expected payment schedule against which actual payments are
recorded over time; and the accumulation of these payments determines the
payment status of the associated agreement. Documents, such as contracts and
receipts, are attached to the relevant project, property, sale, rental, or
customer record, and the system continuously evaluates this data to raise
notifications when a defined condition is met, for example an approaching
contract expiry date or an overdue payment. This conceptual framework,
illustrated in the entity relationship diagram presented in Figure 4.3,
directly shaped both the database design and the business logic implemented=
 in
the system's service layer, discussed in Sections 4.5 and 4.6.</p>

<h1><span style=3D'mso-fareast-font-family:"Times New Roman";mso-font-kerni=
ng:
18.0pt'>Chapter Three: Methodology<o:p></o:p></span></h1>

<h2><span style=3D'mso-fareast-font-family:"Times New Roman"'>3.1 Research
methodology<o:p></o:p></span></h2>

<p>This study followed a design-based research methodology, in which the
primary output is a working artefact, the Real Estate Project Management Sy=
stem
itself, developed and refined through repeated cycles of requirement analys=
is,
design, implementation, and evaluation. Design-based research is appropriate
for information systems projects of this kind because it allows requirement=
s to
be adjusted as understanding of the problem domain deepens, rather than fix=
ing
every requirement before any implementation work begins, which is rarely
realistic for a system whose future users could only be observed informally
rather than engaged as a formal client. An Agile-inspired, iterative
development approach was therefore combined with the overall design-based
research strategy, and is described further in Section 3.5.</p>

<h2><span style=3D'mso-fareast-font-family:"Times New Roman"'>3.2 Tools and
technologies used<o:p></o:p></span></h2>

<p>The system was implemented using the technology stack summarised in Table
3.1. The back end and front end were both built using ASP.NET Core 9, a
cross-platform, open-source web framework maintained by Microsoft, using the
Model-View-Controller, commonly abbreviated as MVC, architectural pattern. =
Data
access was implemented using Entity Framework Core 9 with a Code-First
approach, in which the database schema is generated automatically from stro=
ngly
typed C# entity classes and evolved over time using migrations. Microsoft S=
QL
Server was selected as the relational database management system because of=
 its
strong integration with the .NET ecosystem and its suitability for the
Windows-based deployment environment available for this project. User inter=
face
pages were built with Razor views styled using Bootstrap 5 and Bootstrap Ic=
ons,
and dashboard charts were rendered using Chart.js. ASP.NET Core Identity
supplied user registration, authentication, and role-based authorisation.
Server-side PDF export was implemented using QuestPDF, and spreadsheet expo=
rt
was implemented using ClosedXML, both of which are widely used open-source =
.NET
libraries.</p>

