<h2><span style=3D'mso-fareast-font-family:"Times New Roman"'>3.3 Data coll=
ection
methods<o:p></o:p></span></h2>

<p>Requirements for the system were gathered mainly through informal,
unstructured interviews with individuals familiar with real estate office
operations, supplemented by direct observation of the spreadsheets and paper
forms currently used to track projects, properties, and customer payments. =
This
qualitative approach was chosen because no formal client organisation was
available to complete detailed written requirement questionnaires within the
academic timeframe of the project; instead, the researcher relied on domain
knowledge built up through these conversations and observations, cross-chec=
ked
against the functionality offered by the commercial platforms reviewed in
Section 2.2. Document review of sample sales contracts, rental agreements, =
and
payment receipts also informed the fields captured in the Sale, Rental,
Payment, and Document entities described in Chapter Four.</p>

<h2><span style=3D'mso-fareast-font-family:"Times New Roman"'>3.4 Data anal=
ysis techniques<o:p></o:p></span></h2>

<p>The information gathered during data collection was analysed qualitative=
ly
rather than statistically, given the exploratory nature of the
requirements-gathering process. Recurring themes, such as the repeated
difficulty of tracking outstanding balances by hand and the absence of any
automatic warning for expiring contracts, were extracted from the interview
notes and observations and translated directly into functional requirements,
listed in Table 4.7. Where two candidate designs were being compared, for
example choosing between storing a payment status as a stored value or as a
value calculated on demand, a lightweight cost-benefit comparison was used,
weighing implementation complexity against data consistency; this particular
example is resolved in Section 4.6, where the decision to recalculate payme=
nt
status automatically after every payment transaction is explained.</p>

<h2><span style=3D'mso-fareast-font-family:"Times New Roman"'>3.5 System de=
sign
approach<o:p></o:p></span></h2>

<p>The system was designed and built using an iterative, Agile-inspired
software development life cycle rather than a strict sequential Waterfall
model, because the requirements were expected to, and did, evolve as early
versions of each module were reviewed. As illustrated in Figure 3.1, each
iteration proceeded through five stages: requirements gathering for the mod=
ule
in question, design of the relevant entities, controllers, and views,
implementation of the corresponding code, testing of the resulting
functionality against the business rules identified for that module, and a
review step in which the outcome was compared against the objectives in Sec=
tion
1.3 before the next module was started. This approach allowed early modules,
such as Property and Customer management, to directly inform the design of
later, more dependent modules, such as Sales, Rentals, and Payments, which
build upon the two former entities. Stellman and Greene (2014) describe this
style of incremental delivery as particularly well suited to small teams and
single-developer projects, since it produces a usable, demonstrable increme=
nt of
the system after every cycle rather than only at the very end of the projec=
t.</p>

