<h3><span style=3D'mso-fareast-font-family:"Times New Roman"'>4.1.2
Non-functional requirements<o:p></o:p></span></h3>

<p>In addition to the functional requirements above, the system was designe=
d to
satisfy several non-functional requirements. With respect to security,
passwords are never stored in plain text, being instead hashed by ASP.NET C=
ore
Identity, and every controller action is protected by role-based authorisat=
ion
so that, for example, only staff in the Accountant or Administrator roles m=
ay
record payments. With respect to usability, the interface was built using t=
he
Bootstrap 5 framework to ensure a consistent, responsive layout across desk=
top
and tablet screen sizes. With respect to maintainability, business logic was
placed in dedicated service classes, described in Section 4.6, rather than
directly inside controllers, so that individual modules can be modified with
limited risk of unintended side effects elsewhere in the system. With respe=
ct
to reliability, critical operations such as recording a sale and its associ=
ated
deposit payment are performed within the same logical unit of work, so that=
 the
property status and the payment record are always kept consistent with one
another.</p>

<h2><span style=3D'mso-fareast-font-family:"Times New Roman"'>4.2 System
architecture<o:p></o:p></span></h2>

<p>The system follows a layered, three-tier architecture, illustrated in Fi=
gure
4.1. The presentation layer consists of Razor views rendered by the browser,
styled with Bootstrap 5 and enhanced with Chart.js visualisations and a
reusable notification-bell view component. The application, or business log=
ic,
layer consists of MVC controllers, one for each major module, together with
dedicated service classes that encapsulate the business rules governing sal=
es,
rentals, payments, notifications, dashboard statistics, file storage, and
report export; ASP.NET Core Identity also resides in this layer, governing
authentication and authorisation. The data access layer consists of a gener=
ic
repository abstraction, defined by the IRepository&lt;T&gt; interface and i=
ts
Repository&lt;T&gt; implementation, together with Entity Framework Core's
ApplicationDbContext, which mediates all communication with the underlying
Microsoft SQL Server database. Uploaded files, such as property images and
supporting documents, are stored on the local file system under the
wwwroot/uploads directory and served as static files rather than being stor=
ed
as binary data inside the database, which keeps the database itself compact=
 and
improves the performance of file retrieval.</p>

<div style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt'>

<p class=3DMsoNormal align=3Dcenter style=3D'text-align:center;line-height:=
normal'><span
style=3D'mso-fareast-font-family:"Times New Roman";mso-no-proof:yes'><!--[i=
f gte vml 1]><v:shape
