alt=3D"Architecture diagram" v:shapes=3D"Picture_x0020_2"><![endif]></span>=
<span
style=3D'mso-fareast-font-family:"Times New Roman"'><o:p></o:p></span></p>

</div>

<p class=3Dfigcap>Figure 4.1: Three-tier architecture of the system</p>

<h2><span style=3D'mso-fareast-font-family:"Times New Roman"'>4.3 Use case
diagram<o:p></o:p></span></h2>

<p>Figure 4.2 presents the use case diagram of the system, showing the five
actors identified during requirements analysis, namely Administrator, Proje=
ct
Manager, Sales Agent, Accountant, and Customer, together with the principal=
 use
cases each actor may perform. The Administrator has access to the complete =
set
of use cases, including user and role management, while the remaining staff
roles are restricted to the use cases relevant to their responsibilities; f=
or
example, the Sales Agent may record a sale but may not manage user accounts,
and the Accountant may record payments but may not create new properties. T=
he
Customer actor is limited to viewing the sale or rental agreement associate=
d with
that customer's own account. Every actor participates in the Login and
Authenticate use case, since role-based access control is enforced at every
controller action.</p>

<div style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt'>

<p class=3DMsoNormal align=3Dcenter style=3D'text-align:center;line-height:=
normal'><span
style=3D'mso-fareast-font-family:"Times New Roman";mso-no-proof:yes'><!--[i=
f gte vml 1]><v:shape
 id=3D"Picture_x0020_3" o:spid=3D"_x0000_i1041" type=3D"#_x0000_t75" alt=3D=
"Use case diagram"
 style=3D'width:1020pt;height:660pt;visibility:visible;mso-wrap-style:squar=
e'>
 <v:imagedata src=3D"Real_Estate_Project_Management_System_Thesis_files/ima=
ge005.png"
  o:title=3D"Use case diagram"/>
</v:shape><![endif]--><![if !vml]><img width=3D1360 height=3D880
src=3D"Real_Estate_Project_Management_System_Thesis_files/image006.jpg"
alt=3D"Use case diagram" v:shapes=3D"Picture_x0020_3"><![endif]></span><span
style=3D'mso-fareast-font-family:"Times New Roman"'><o:p></o:p></span></p>

</div>

<p class=3Dfigcap>Figure 4.2: Use case diagram of the system</p>

<h2><span style=3D'mso-fareast-font-family:"Times New Roman"'>4.4 Data flow=
 and
entity relationship diagram<o:p></o:p></span></h2>

<p>At a data flow level, a new property is created within the context of an
existing project; when a sale or a rental is subsequently recorded against =
that
property, the property's status is updated automatically and an initial pay=
ment
is generated if a deposit was specified; every additional payment recorded
against that sale or rental triggers a recalculation of the outstanding bal=
ance
and the corresponding payment status. Figure 4.3 presents the simplified en=
tity
relationship diagram that models these flows at the database level. Each
project may contain many properties and many project tasks, and is optional=
ly
supervised by one application user acting as project manager. Each property=
 may
be associated with, at most, one active sale and one active rental at any g=
iven
time, reflecting the business rule that a single property cannot be sold and
rented simultaneously. Each sale and each rental may accumulate many payment
records over time, and each customer may be linked to many sales, rentals, =
and
payments across the lifetime of the system. Documents and notifications are
attached as supporting entities linked, respectively, to the relevant
transactional record and to the application user who should receive the
notification.</p>

<div style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt'>

<p class=3DMsoNormal align=3Dcenter style=3D'text-align:center;line-height:=
normal'><span
